```tsx
import { Component, type ErrorInfo, type ReactNode } from "react";
import { invoke } from "@tauri-apps/api/core";
import { reportError } from "../lib/errorLog";
import { FolioButton } from "../components/FolioButton";

interface Props {
  children: ReactNode;
  /** "root": reload + log folder. "reader": back-to-library recovery. */
  variant: "root" | "reader";
  onRecover?: () => void; // reader: close doc + navigate to library
}

interface State { error: Error | null }
```

componentDidCatch(error, info): reportError(error, "render.boundary"); also include componentStack in the log message. reportError takes error + context; to include the component stack, wrap: create an Error with stack = `${message}\n${componentStack}`. Simpler: `reportError(new Error(...))`? We lose the original stack. Compose: 

```ts
const composed = error instanceof Error ? error : new Error(String(error));
// append component stack
```
Better: reportError with error, then a second line for component stack: reportError accepts any value → describe stringifies. I'll do:

```ts
reportError(error, this.props.variant === "reader" ? "render.reader" : "render.root");
if (info.componentStack) reportError({ componentStack: info.componentStack }, "render.stack");
```
Hmm two log lines. Cleaner: augment the error's stack once:

```ts
if (error instanceof Error && info.componentStack) {
  error.stack = `${error.stack ?? ""}\nComponent stack:${info.componentStack}`;
}
reportError(error, ctx);
```
Mutating stack is fine here.

UI: recovery screen with plain-language text, buttons:
- root: Reload (window.location.reload()), Open log folder (invoke open_log_dir), Copy diagnostics (clipboard write error + build info; fallback execCommand).
- reader: Back to library (onRecover + flush handled by parent), Reload, Copy diagnostics.

Copy diagnostics: build a string with version, userAgent, error message/stack. navigator.clipboard may fail → fallback textarea select/copy.

